/// A node's code after its start: a compiled module loaded by the program, a
/// module's definitions by their hashes, and the processes running a function.
///
/// A program uses it to move what runs to new code without a restart: it loads
/// a module built after the node started, and sends each process that runs an
/// old function the message by which the process replaces its own code (report
/// §6.10). Code that a peer lacks crosses with a spawn, and needs no load
/// (report §8.7).
///
/// A unit a load makes is held for the node's life, as every unit is, and adds
/// to the host's counts of module names, export entries, function entries and
/// atoms, which never fall while the node runs (report §11.2).
///
/// `running` is a form, as `Peer.spawn` is: it is called where it is named, with
/// the name of a top-level `fn` whose result type is `Unit`, and answers
/// the processes that run that function at the moment of the call (report
/// §3.11).
///
/// ## Examples
///
/// Every process that runs `count` sent the message by which it moves to
/// `count2`, which counts each `Inc` twice, its total kept; here no process
/// runs `count`, and none is sent one:
///
/// ```ernest
/// type CounterMsg =
///     Inc(Int)
///   | Get(reply : Reply(Int))
///   | Upgrade(migrate : (Int) -> Int, next : (Int) -> Unit receiving CounterMsg)
///
/// fn count(total : Int) : Unit receiving CounterMsg =
///     receive {
///         Inc(amount) -> count(total + amount)
///       | Get(reply = reply) -> {
///             answer(reply, total);
///             count(total)
///         }
///       | Upgrade(migrate = migrate, next = next) -> next(migrate(total))
///     }
///
/// fn count2(total : Int) : Unit receiving CounterMsg =
///     receive {
///         Inc(amount) -> count2(total + 2 * amount)
///       | Get(reply = reply) -> {
///             answer(reply, total);
///             count2(total)
///         }
///       | Upgrade(migrate = migrate, next = next) -> next(migrate(total))
///     }
///
/// List.forEach(Code.running(count),
///              fn(process) = send(process, Upgrade(migrate = fn(n) = n, next = count2)))
/// // => Unit
/// ```
///
/// ## See also
///
/// `Peer.spawn`, which carries a function to a peer that lacks it, and
/// `Process.live`, every process the runtime runs.
///
/// since 0.4.0

/// Loads the compiled module at `path`, with the modules it uses, and evaluates
/// the top-level bindings among them that have no value on this node, in the
/// calling process, and answers `Right(Unit)`. The modules it uses are found by
/// their namespaces under the module's own root and then on the load path, as
/// `ern run` finds a program's (report §11.2), and each definition is verified
/// against its hash. A module the node holds with the same code is the node's
/// own; one it holds with other code, another build's, is loaded beside it, and
/// a process that runs the other goes on running it. The bindings are evaluated
/// in the order `ern run` evaluates a program's before `main`, each in a body of
/// mailbox type `Never` (report §8.5).
///
/// A file that is no `.erc` of this `ern`, or that was compiled against another
/// interface than the node holds, answers `Left(Invalid)`, and a module the load
/// path lacks `Left(NotFound)`. A binding that faults answers `Left(Other(text))`,
/// `text` the fault as `ern run` reports it, `Init.bad:3 faulted: division by
/// zero`: nothing is loaded, and every process the evaluation started is killed.
/// So does a load the node cannot make, `text` its reason, a count of the host's
/// that the load would pass among them.
///
/// ### Examples
///
/// ```ernest
/// Code.load(Path("nowhere/counter.erc"))
/// // => Left(NotFound)
/// ```
export foreign fn load(path : Path) : Either(Io.Error, Unit) =
    "ern_code_load:load/1"

/// The definitions the compiled module at `path` holds, each as its qualified
/// name and its hash, the digest in lowercase hexadecimal, in the order the
/// module declares them. A foreign declaration has no hash and is left out. A
/// file that is no `.erc` of this `ern` answers `Left(Invalid)`, and a path that
/// names no file `Left(NotFound)`.
///
/// ### Examples
///
/// ```ernest
/// Code.hashes(Path("nowhere/counter.erc"))
/// // => Left(NotFound)
/// ```
export foreign fn hashes(path : Path) : Either(Io.Error, List(#(String, String))) =
    "ern_code:hashes/1"

/// The addresses of the processes on this node whose stack holds a frame of `f`
/// itself at the moment of the call, in unspecified order. `f` is the name of a
/// top-level `fn` of any arity whose result type is `Unit`; where it receives,
/// `receiving m`, `m` holds no type variable, and every process listed has the
/// mailbox type `m`. A process spawned from a lambda written in `f` runs that
/// lambda, and is not listed. The list is a snapshot: a process may have left `f`, or ended, by
/// the time it is sent to. The host keeps at most the innermost 64 frames of a
/// stack, so a process whose frame of `f` lies beneath them is not listed.
/// `running` is called where it is named, and is no value (report §3.11).
///
/// ### Examples
///
/// ```ernest
/// fn idle() : Unit receiving Int =
///     receive {
///         _ -> idle()
///     }
///
/// Code.running(idle)
/// // => []
/// ```
export foreign fn running(f : a) : List(Address(m)) =
    "ern_code:running/1"
